window.docContents[151].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations":{"contents":"Hyperbolic equations carry information at finite speed. Where the heat equation\nspreads a bump of data over the whole domain at once, a plucked string splits into\na left-moving half and a right-moving half, and the value at a point depends only\non the data in a cone beneath it. In nonlinear transport\nand conservation laws the characteristic speed depends on the solution itself, so\nfor suitable decreasing initial data the characteristics of Burgers's equation\n run into one another, the gradient steepens without bound in finite\ntime, and the classical solution simply stops existing. The theory then has to\ndecide what the word \"solution\" means on the other side of that breakdown.\n\nDifferent equations need different solution concepts. Distributional\nconservation laws admit more than one weak solution, so entropy inequalities are\nbrought in to select among them. Hamilton–Jacobi solutions crease rather than\njump, so viscosity inequalities replace derivatives that are not there. Weak waves\nwant energy spaces and second-order evolution, and symmetric systems keep their\nmatrix structure in first-order form. Each concept comes with the\ncalculations that support it: explicit formulas checked against\ntheir residuals and initial traces, energy identities yielding uniqueness and\nfinite propagation, the Rankine–Hugoniot and entropy conditions sorting admissible\ndiscontinuities from inadmissible ones, and variational formulas related to\nviscosity solutions under stated hypotheses.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas","header":"7. Hyperbolic Equations","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Trace--Assembly-on-an-Operational-C___-Domain":{"contents":"Boundary charts reduce each localized piece of a regular domain to the flat\nmodel. Surface-measure comparison transports the half-space estimate back to\nthe physical boundary, and the finite partition of unity assembles one global\noperator. The hypotheses expose every chart, cutoff, and quantitative bound\nused in that argument.\n\nFor any set , the intrinsic surface measure is\nthe normalized codimension-one Euclidean Hausdorff measure restricted to the\ntopological frontier:The normalization is Mathlib's, for which  on \nis Lebesgue measure, so on a flat hyperplane  is Lebesgue\nmeasure in tangential coordinates. The definition needs no regularity of\n; in dimension  the codimension  is truncated to  by\nnatural subtraction, a case nothing uses. Every trace operator on an\noperational  domain below takes values in \nor  for this measure. The boundary-calculus\nchapter proves that it has finite total mass on bounded operational \ndomains and compares it with the chart-assembled atlas measure.\n\nFor the intrinsic measure to serve as the boundary measure on a general\ndomain, it must agree with tangential Lebesgue measure on the flat model,\nwhere the trace theorems above were proved.\n\nLet  and let\n, , be the\ninclusion of the boundary hyperplane. Then\n,  is an isometry onto\nthis hyperplane, andthe pushforward of Lebesgue measure  on .\nConsequently, for every ,as Bochner integrals, with no integrability hypothesis on , and every\nbounded measurable subset of the hyperplane has finite surface measure.\n\nThe frontier of the open half-space is computed directly from the last\ncoordinate. The inclusion  preserves distances, and an isometry\npushes normalized Hausdorff measure of any dimension forward to the same\nHausdorff measure restricted to its range; since  on\n is Lebesgue measure and the range of  is the\nhyperplane, this is the pushforward identity. The integral formula is the\nchange of variables along the closed measurable embedding . A\nbounded set has bounded preimage under the isometry, hence finite Lebesgue\nmeasure.\n\nWith the two measures identified, the flat trace can be rewritten so that its\nvalues are functions on the boundary hyperplane rather than of the tangential\ncoordinate. Composition with the measure-preserving projection is an isometry\nof  spaces, so the norm estimate is unchanged.\n\nLet  drop the last coordinate. It is\nmeasure preserving from the flat surface measure\n to Lebesgue measure on\n, so for every  composition with \nis a linear isometryComposing the flat trace of  with\n gives the intrinsic flat trace\n\nThe intrinsic flat trace is the local model. On a domain with an operational\natlas, each boundary cutoff piece of a function is flattened by its chart,\ntraced on the half-space, and transported back to the physical boundary; the\nfinite partition of unity sums the pieces.\n\nFor an operational bounded  domain\n, the library constructs a continuous linear\nmapHere  is the intrinsic codimension-one Hausdorff measure restricted\nto the frontier; the charts enter the operator, not the measure. This is the\nexponent-two case of Evans's theorem. The C1ExtensionAtlas hypothesis\nsupplies global chart extensions, cutoffs and a finite partition of unity. For a\nbounded open set with  graph boundary,\nsuch an atlas exists. It also builds\nthe global extension operator of the preceding chapter, but the trace is\nassembled directly from localized flattening maps and flat traces.\n\nThe assembled operator is a finite sum of completed pieces, so it is\ncharacterized by the same two facts as the flat model: agreement with literal\nrestriction on ambient smooth functions and a norm estimate. Agreement is now\nin  with respect to the intrinsic surface\nmeasure.\n\nFor every globally smooth compactly supported ambient function, the assembled\ntrace agrees in boundary  with literal restriction to\n.Agreement also holds beyond the smooth core. Let  and let\n have a representative continuous on\n. Then the finite-exponent trace \nconstructed below equals the boundary restriction of that representative\n-almost everywhere, and so does  when .\nThese statements assume an inward partition of unity, a finite family of\ncutoffs with a fixed inward direction on each patch. Such a partition exists\non bounded open  domains, and one version of the statement assumes only\na bounded open set with  graph boundary.\n\nLocalize the ambient smooth function by the finite boundary partition of\nunity. Flattening with a  chart and a target cutoff gives a compactly\nsupported  function, so the compact- flat-trace agreement theorem\nidentifies its boundary value. The chart's surface-measure pushforward bound\ntransports this almost-everywhere identity back to the physical patch.\nSumming the partition pieces yields literal boundary restriction in\n.For a continuous representative, inward mollification gives smooth\napproximants converging both in  and pointwise on\n. Their traces converge in boundary , so a\nsubsequence converges almost everywhere there. Smooth agreement identifies\nthis limit with the representative's boundary restriction.\n\nThe estimate inherits the unit constant of the flat trace, so each chart\ncontributes only its flattening norm and its surface-measure distortion.\n\nThe trace norm is bounded by a finite sum of atlas-dependent chart constants:Here  is the continuous linear flattening operator for the th\nlocalized chart and  is its surface-measure distortion bound. Thus the\nmap in  is quantitatively continuous.\n\nEach flattened boundary piece satisfies the half-space trace estimate.  The\nsurface-measure pushforward bound transports that estimate to the physical\nboundary, and the triangle inequality sums over the finite chart cover.\n\nThe kernel-quotient construction of the flat case depends only on having a\nbounded operator out of a Hilbert space, so it applies verbatim to the\nassembled trace.\n\nThe intrinsic Hilbert boundary space of an operational  domain isIt carries the quotient norm inherited from , and the induced\nboundary-value mapis injective. Hence this quotient realizes precisely the range of the\nassembled trace inside boundary . The Fourier chapter identifies it,\nwith two-sided norm bounds, with a space of compatible chartwise \ndata, and shows that two atlases give equivalent chartwise spaces.\n\nOrthogonal decomposition in  again produces the canonical\nleast-energy representative of each trace class. Minimality and uniqueness\ncome from Hilbert-space geometry alone, not from the charts.\n\nDefine  by applying the same representative map to the assembled\ntrace . The quotient theorem gives, for every class ,This extension has least  norm among all functions with the\nsame boundary value and is the unique such representative orthogonal to the\nzero-trace kernel.\n\nFor  the same chart assembly is run with the finite-exponent flat\ntrace as the local model. The parallel with the Hilbert case is exact except\nfor the constants and for the absence of an inner product on the quotient.\n\nFor every real  and every operational bounded  domain\n, the chart construction gives a continuous\nlinear mapThe source is the general completed Sobolev graph space, and the target uses\nintrinsic codimension-one Hausdorff measure on the frontier.\n\nClassical agreement and the norm estimate are inherited from the flat\nfinite-exponent trace exactly as in the Hilbert case, with the flat\nconstant  now appearing in each chart term alongside .\n\nThe completed operator agrees with literal boundary restriction on every\nambient smooth compactly supported function. It also satisfieswhere  is the chart-flattening operator and  is the corresponding\nsurface-measure distortion bound.\n\nLocalize by the finite boundary partition, flatten each piece, and apply the\nfinite-exponent half-space trace estimate. The chart pushforward inequality\ntransports the local trace back with factor . On smooth input,\nthe compact- agreement theorem identifies each contribution with\n on the physical boundary. Summing the partition gives literal\nrestriction; summing the local norm estimates gives the stated bound.\n\nThe intrinsic trace space is again the quotient by the kernel, as in the flat\ncase.\n\nFor an operational  domain and , the intrinsic trace\nspace isThis Banach quotient maps continuously and injectively into\n, with image exactly\n. It is linearly equivalent to\nthat algebraic range. For  the range is characterized\nchartwise:  is a trace if and only if every localized chart coordinate of\n lies in the Gagliardo space of order , and compatible chart data\nlift boundedly to . The range is thus identified as a set,\ntogether with a bounded lifting, rather than through a norm equivalence\nbetween  and a chartwise Gagliardo space.\n\nAs on the half-space, the orthogonal-complement construction needs an inner\nproduct. The condition a bounded right inverse must satisfy is the same.\n\nA bounded linear map\n splits the quotient map\nif and only iffor every intrinsic trace class , where  is the injective\nboundary realization.\n\nFactor the trace through the quotient map and the injective boundary-value\nrealization. A right inverse immediately gives the trace identity. Conversely,\nthe trace identity becomes equality after applying the boundary realization,\nwhose injectivity recovers the quotient identity.\n\nFor , the local fractional traces and the lifting give such a\nright inverse. Write  and\n. Each  coordinate of  is the\nlocalized chart coordinate of  almost everywhere.\nSince a Gagliardo element is determined by its  coordinate,  vanishes on\n. Thus the bounded linear map  descends\nthrough the quotient to\n\n\n\nCompatibility of the chart data  with the physical trace gives\n.\nThe criterion above shows that  splits the quotient. This splitting is\nderived here from the maps above by quotient descent, and it requires\n.\n\nThe two assembled operators overlap at , and their compatibility follows\nthe flat pattern: the canonical exponent identification of the domain carries\none operator to the other, hence one kernel and one quotient to the other.\n\nFor every operational  domain, the finite-exponent trace at \ncommutes with the canonical identificationsConsequently  identifies the zero-trace kernels and induces a\ncontinuous linear equivalencecompatible with quotient classes and their intrinsic boundary values.\n\nThe finite and Hilbert operators agree on ambient smooth compactly supported\nfunctions after exponent transport. Density of that core in \nupgrades the agreement to equality of continuous linear maps. Kernel\ncompatibility follows from injectivity of the boundary exponent equivalence,\nand the generic kernel-quotient construction supplies the trace-space\nequivalence and its commuting boundary-value identity.\n\nEverything above was built from one atlas: its charts, flattening operators\nand partition of unity all enter the constant in the trace bound. The\noperator itself does not depend on those choices. Since the operator is\nassembled chart by chart, this has to be proved. Two continuous\nlinear maps that agree on a dense set are equal, and every atlas produces a\nmap that is literal restriction on smooth functions.\n\nLet . A continuous linear map\n that sends every\nrestriction of an ambient smooth compactly supported function to its\nboundary values is the trace . In particular, two\noperational  atlases on the same domain construct the same continuous\nlinear trace. Both statements also hold for functions with values in a real\nBanach space.\n\nFor finite , restrictions of ambient smooth compactly supported functions\nare dense in : extend with any one atlas, mollify in the\nwhole space, and restrict. On that set  and  both\ngive literal restriction, by hypothesis and by classical agreement. The\ntarget is built from intrinsic surface measure, which involves no chart, so\nthe two maps have the same codomain and agree everywhere by continuity.\nFor two atlases, apply the first statement to the trace built from the\nfirst atlas, with the second atlas defining .\n\nThe same chart assembly works for functions with values in a real Banach\nspace . The half-space trace of an -valued function is built from the\nendpoint inequality for norms, and the charts transport it exactly as in the\nscalar case. For  it reproduces the scalar trace, and it\ncommutes with every bounded linear map , so a trace can be taken\nbefore or after applying  pointwise.\n\nLet  be a real Banach space, , and  an open\nset carrying an operational  atlas. There is a continuous linear mapwith the same chart-sum bound as the scalar trace. If the base coordinate of\n agrees almost everywhere on  with an ambient  function\n, then ;  need not have\ncompact support. For every bounded linear  into a real Banach\nspace,and for  the trace agrees with the scalar\n under the identification of the two graph spaces.\nThe flat half-space trace has the same properties, with a compactly\nsupported  representative in the classical formula.\n\nThe operator is the finite sum over charts of localize, flatten, take the\nhalf-space trace, and transport back to surface measure. Each summand is\nbounded, which gives the bound. On a  representative each summand is\nthe restriction of , and the cutoffs sum to one on the boundary;\nthe cutoffs also supply the compact support the flat theorem needs. For\nnaturality, both sides are continuous in  and agree on ambient smooth\ncompactly supported functions by the classical formula, which are dense.\nScalar compatibility is the same density argument, since the scalar\nidentification preserves every Sobolev coordinate and the graph norm.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings\u0009Trace","header":"4.3.2.2. Assembly on an Operational C¹ Domain","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Trace--Assembly-on-an-Operational-C___-Domain"}});